//! lotus/deess.rye — ALES64: the de-esser — the first sidechain COMPOSITION. ALES63 gave the detector its freedom: a compressor
//! keyed by a SEPARATE signal rather than the track it turns down, and named the three tools that freedom unlocks — the de-esser (a
//! band-pass key), the ducker (a voice key), the keyed gate. This rung builds the first of them from proven parts, adding no new
//! arithmetic on the audio path. A de-esser tames the harsh "ess" of a vocal without dulling the whole take: it listens to only the
//! SIBILANT BAND (the high frequencies where the ess lives), and when that band gets loud it ducks the WHOLE vocal for that instant,
//! so the ess softens while the body of the voice stays bright. That is exactly a sidechain whose key is a BAND-PASSED COPY of the
//! signal itself: band-pass a copy to isolate the sibilant band (ALES46's proven Bandpass), then key ALES63's compressor on that copy
//! over the original (ALES63's compress_key_follow). The de-esser IS that composition — band-pass a copy, then sidechain-compress —
//! so it inherits every proof beneath it: the band-pass is the two proven one-pole stages in cascade, and the keyed compressor is the
//! ordinary attack/hold/release compressor generalized to a key. The threshold is crossed by the KEY's band-passed level, not the
//! target's broadband level, so a loud low note passes untouched while a quiet ess ducks — the de-esser reacts to the band, not the
//! volume.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES64.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-185604_fill-ales64-lotus-de-esser.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, samples(), splice, the range law shape; lotus/band.rye (ALES46) — the Bandpass
//! that isolates the sibilant band, its run the two proven one-pole stages in cascade; lotus/sidechain.rye (ALES63) — the keyed
//! compressor this drives, its compress_key_follow reading the band-passed copy as the detector while the gain lands on the target.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the band cutoffs are one-pole coefficients, the attack,
//! release, and hold counted in sample INDICES, not milliseconds; the threshold is a magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain
//! fraction; there is no lookahead. One whole-span pass over the copy and the target; the from-silence form only (a carried/spanning
//! de-esser, threading the band-pass and follower states together, is a later rung, exactly as the base dynamics preceded their
//! carried forms). The band-passed copy lives in a caller-owned scratch clip, so nothing on the path allocates.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/deess.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const tone = @import("tone.rye");
const band = @import("band.rye");
const sidechain = @import("sidechain.rye");

pub const deess_version = "20260814.185604";

/// The de-esser's faults, one consistent name each, merged from the two proven tools it composes: the band-pass contributes BadCoeff
/// (an illegal band cutoff) and BadRange (a span outside the samples); the keyed compressor contributes BadThreshold, BadRatio,
/// BadCoeff (an illegal attack or release coefficient), BadHold, BadRange, and BadKey — every name coinciding with the family's, so a
/// composer sees one consistent fault surface. BadKey cannot arise here (the scratch key is a full copy of the target, so it always
/// covers the span); it is named only because it rides in on the sidechain's set.
pub const DeessError = tone.ToneError || sidechain.SidechainError;

/// De-ess `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `target`: band-pass a copy of the target into the caller-owned scratch
/// `key` to isolate the sibilant band (the high-pass at `num_low/den_low` drops the lows, the low-pass at `num_high/den_high` drops
/// the highs — ALES46's Bandpass), then key ALES63's attack/hold/release compressor on that copy over the target (ALES63's
/// compress_key_follow), from a fresh envelope with the hold unarmed. The gain lands on the raw target while the detector listens to
/// the band-passed copy, so the threshold is crossed by the SIBILANT band's level, not the target's broadband level — a loud low
/// note whose sibilant band is quiet passes untouched, a loud ess ducks the whole voice for that instant. The `key` clip must arrive
/// EMPTY; the de-esser fills it with the band-passed copy, so nothing on the path allocates. Refuses BadCoeff (either band cutoff or
/// an illegal attack/release coefficient), BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadHold, and BadRange before the target is touched — the band-pass
/// runs first over the scratch copy, so an illegal band refuses before a single target sample changes, and the keyed compressor
/// checks all of its own edges before any write. On any refusal the target is untouched (the scratch key may hold a partial copy —
/// it is scratch). Target values only — never a length.
pub fn deess(
    target: *timeline.Clip,
    key: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    num_low: i32,
    den_low: u32,
    num_high: i32,
    den_high: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) DeessError!void {
    // invariant: the scratch key arrives empty — the de-esser fills it with a band-passed copy of the target and owns its whole
    // content, so a caller never has to size or clear it and nothing here allocates.
    assert(key.len == 0);

    // Build the key: an exact copy of the target, then the sibilant band isolated over the span. splice into the empty scratch makes
    // key.len == target.len, so the key covers the span and BadKey can never fire below.
    timeline.splice(key, 0, target.samples()) catch {
        // splice of a full copy into an empty clip cannot exceed max_clip (target already fits), so this branch names an impossible
        // state defensively rather than a reachable fault; map it onto the range fault the family already knows.
        return error.BadRange;
    };
    // invariant: the key is now an exact, same-length copy of the target — the detector will read the same span the gain lands on.
    assert(key.len == target.len);

    // Isolate the sibilant band in the copy. Its precheck refuses an illegal cutoff (BadCoeff) or span (BadRange) before it writes,
    // and it touches only the scratch — the target is still whole here.
    var sibilant = band.Bandpass{ .num_low = num_low, .den_low = den_low, .num_high = num_high, .den_high = den_high };
    try sibilant.run(key, start, count);

    // Key the attack/hold/release compressor on the band-passed copy over the target. Every threshold, ratio, coefficient, hold, and
    // range edge is checked before any target write, so a fault here leaves the target exactly as it was.
    try sidechain.compress_key_follow(target, key, start, count, threshold, ratio_num, ratio_den, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);

    // invariant: a de-esser changes target sample values only, never the target's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= target.len);
    assert(target.len <= target.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: silence stays silence; a de-esser IS the composition {band-pass a copy; sidechain-compress} byte-for-byte (the
//     grounding law, run by hand); the KEY's band-passed level sets the threshold crossing (a threshold just above the band-passed
//     peak is the identity, a threshold below it ducks — proven by MEASURING the copy's peak, so the sibilant band, not the broadband
//     level, drives the gain); a loud in-band signal is ducked with the sign held and never expanding; and each fault refuses by name
//     before the target is touched -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. silence stays silence — an all-zero target de-esses to all zeros: the band-passed copy is silent, so nothing crosses.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE GROUNDING LAW — a de-esser IS the composition {band-pass a copy of the target; then sidechain-compress the target keyed
    //    on that copy}, byte-for-byte. Run the two steps by hand and demand equality: the de-esser adds no arithmetic on the audio
    //    path — it is ALES46's band-pass and ALES63's keyed compressor in composition.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 6000, -5800, 6200, 5900, -6100, 5700, 12000, -11800, 9000, 500, -900, 1100 };
        // the de-esser
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &sig);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 3000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2);
        // the composition by hand: band-pass a copy, then key the ALES63 compressor on it over a fresh copy of the signal
        var hand_key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&hand_key, &sig);
        var f = band.Bandpass{ .num_low = 1, .den_low = 3, .num_high = 1, .den_high = 2 };
        try f.run(&hand_key, 0, hand_key.len);
        var hand_target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&hand_target, &sig);
        try sidechain.compress_key_follow(&hand_target, &hand_key, 0, hand_target.len, 3000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2);
        if (!eq(target.samples(), hand_target.samples())) return error.NotComposition;
    }

    // 3. THE KEY'S BAND-PASSED LEVEL SETS THE CROSSING — measure the band-passed copy's peak P, then a threshold just ABOVE P is the
    //    identity (nothing crosses, the target passes byte-for-byte however loud its broadband level), while a threshold BELOW P ducks
    //    (some sample reduced). So it is the SIBILANT band, not the target's raw level, that drives the gain — the de-esser reacts to
    //    the band. Instant attack/release and no hold isolate the crossing from the time base.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9000, -9000, 9000, -9000, 9000, -9000, 9000, -9000 }; // a loud, fast, in-band (sibilant) signal
        // measure the band-passed copy's peak the same way the de-esser builds its key
        var probe = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&probe, &sig);
        var pf = band.Bandpass{ .num_low = 1, .den_low = 3, .num_high = 1, .den_high = 2 };
        try pf.run(&probe, 0, probe.len);
        var peak: i32 = 0;
        {
            const kb = probe.samples();
            var i: u32 = 0;
            while (i < kb.len) : (i += 1) if (abs16(kb[i]) > peak) {
                peak = abs16(kb[i]);
            };
        }
        // invariant: the probe is a real band-passed signal with energy — a legal threshold above it exists and is below the rail.
        assert(peak >= 1 and peak < @as(i32, timeline.sample_max));

        // threshold just above the band-passed peak — nothing crosses, the identity, however loud the broadband signal.
        var above = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&above, &sig);
        var akey = timeline.Clip{};
        try deess(&above, &akey, 0, above.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, peak + 1, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0);
        if (!eq(above.samples(), &sig)) return error.AboveKeyReduced;

        // threshold below the band-passed peak — the key crosses, the whole target ducks: some sample is reduced.
        var below = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&below, &sig);
        var bkey = timeline.Clip{};
        try deess(&below, &bkey, 0, below.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, peak - 1, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0);
        var reduced = false;
        {
            const out = below.samples();
            var i: u32 = 0;
            while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
                if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(sig[i])) return error.DeessExpanded; // never boosts
                if (abs16(out[i]) < abs16(sig[i])) reduced = true;
            }
        }
        if (!reduced) return error.SibilantDidNotDuck;
    }

    // 4. THE SIGN IS HELD AND THE MAGNITUDE NEVER EXPANDS — a loud in-band signal de-esses so every output magnitude is at most the
    //    target input's and every sign matches, under a real band, attack, release, and hold.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 12000, -12000, 6000, -6000, 15000, -15000 };
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &sig);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 2000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const out = target.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(sig[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((sig[i] > 0 and out[i] < 0) or (sig[i] < 0 and out[i] > 0)) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 5a. an illegal band cutoff (a high-pass numerator past the denominator) refuses BadCoeff before the target is touched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadBandSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadBand;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedBandMutated;
    }

    // 5b. a zero threshold refuses BadThreshold before the target is touched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 0, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedThresholdMutated;
    }

    // 5c. a sub-unity ratio refuses BadRatio before the target is touched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRatioMutated;
    }

    // 5d. an illegal attack coefficient refuses BadCoeff before the target is touched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1000, 4, 1, 3, 2, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedAttackMutated;
    }

    // 5e. a hold longer than max_clip refuses BadHold before the target is touched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHoldMutated;
    }

    // 5f. an out-of-range span refuses BadRange before the target is touched (caught by the band-pass over the copy first).
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        if (deess(&target, &key, 2, 5, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-deess: the DE-ESSER — the first sidechain composition: band-pass a copy of the target to isolate the sibilant " ++
        "band (ALES46's Bandpass), then key ALES63's attack/hold/release compressor on that copy over the target. Silence stays " ++
        "silence; the de-esser IS the composition {{band-pass a copy; sidechain-compress}} BYTE-FOR-BYTE (the grounding law, run by " ++
        "hand — it adds no arithmetic on the audio path); the KEY's band-passed level sets the threshold crossing (a threshold just " ++
        "above the band-passed peak is the identity however loud the broadband level, a threshold below it ducks — proven by MEASURING " ++
        "the copy's peak, so the sibilant band, not the raw level, drives the gain); a loud in-band signal ducks with the sign held " ++
        "and never expanding; and an illegal band cutoff refuses BadCoeff, a zero threshold BadThreshold, a sub-unity ratio BadRatio, " ++
        "an illegal attack coefficient BadCoeff, a hold past max_clip BadHold, an out-of-range span BadRange, each before the target " ++
        "is touched; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
